(meta (title "Writing a page")
      (date 2026-09-20))

A page is a @code{.sz} file: text, with commands in it where the text needs
more than text. A command is an @code{@"@"} and a name, perhaps arguments in
brackets, and perhaps a body in braces:

@codeblock|<{
(meta (title "Writing a page")
      (date 2026-09-20))

A page is a @code{.sz} file: text, with @em{commands} in it.@note{Like
this one, which is a footnote.}

@section{A section}

@link["https://example.com"]{A link}, and some code:

@codeblock[#:lang "scheme"]|{
  (println "hello")
}|
}>|

The @code{(meta ...)} at the top is where the title and the date are. They
are what the page's header says, and what the menu of its folder says about
it. A page without them is called by its file's name, and dated by when the
file was last written. A date may be written with quotes or without.

A blank line starts a new paragraph. Quotes curl by themselves, "like this",
and three dashes --- like these --- are a dash.@note{Code is left as it was
typed: @code{"straight" -- quotes}.}

@section{What there is}

@read-list{
- @em{emphasis}, @strong{strong}, @code{code} and @link["menus.html"]{links};
- sections, which are the headings of a page, below its title;
- footnotes, which go to the end;
- code blocks, lists, @link["lists-and-tables.html"]{tables} and quotations;
- @link["markdown.html"]{Markdown}, where it is quicker;
- @code{@"@"include}, for a piece of a page kept in a file of its own.
}

The whole of it is in samizdat's
@link["https://github.com/bjoli/samizdat"]{Readme}.

